{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793717507,"nonce":"5uO84LQzK+SoyDJrjQAIhccNYY0=","message":"Landed in a2ce2c51f7aea6c768fc1da5acceeccc19cd9b87.\n\nFresh constructor receivers now omit redundant receiver-nil checks. Colon-call chains retain the exact receiver class only when every resolved method target returns its unchanged first parameter on every normal return. Callee absence, reassigned/shadowed/captured parameters, alternate or missing results, dynamic lookup, dot-call arguments and pending errors remain conservative.\n\nNUC, i5-1250P, taskset 0,2,4,6, frozen release binaries built with llvm-backend, ZYLUA_LLVM=1 and both backend queries confirmed llvm. Interleaved baseline/candidate/LuaJIT, no Cargo or rustc during samples. Baseline 142b19055560926120418de961de3f24cd334c9d. Two independent 21-sample method_call sets:\n- Set 1 wall: 41.283 -\u003e 38.554 ms; kernel: 7.442 -\u003e 7.499 ms. LuaJIT 6.265 / 4.304 ms.\n- Set 2 wall: 41.122 -\u003e 38.139 ms; kernel: 7.495 -\u003e 7.636 ms. LuaJIT 6.247 / 4.312 ms.\n- Combined 42-sample medians: wall 41.211 -\u003e 38.412 ms (6.8% lower); kernel 7.491 -\u003e 7.530 ms, effectively unchanged. LuaJIT wall 6.255 / kernel 4.306 ms. This is a startup improvement, not a hot-loop speedup.\n\nActual benchmarks/method_call.lua main chunk lowered HIR: 1438 -\u003e 1151 instructions, 406 -\u003e 283 blocks, 159 -\u003e 97 calls, 51 -\u003e 10 zl_index_nil calls. NthToggle.activate remains 305 instructions / 97 blocks / 23 calls; differences in that dump are global IDs, not reduced instructions. It still has 7 nil checks, 4 integer boxing calls, zl_arith and zl_le. Proving field initialization for specialized receivers remains the next hot-path lever, tracked in 53de327; exact class alone does not prove field presence.\n\nAll 17 benchmark checksums matched PUC Lua 5.4. The other 16 kernels were compared with five samples per arm. Eight guard kernels had byte-identical lowered HIR: records, binary_trees, closure_call, free_function_call, inlined_call, lambda_call, op_overload, nbody. A small closure_call wall outlier disappeared in an independent 21-sample repeat (15.620 -\u003e 15.391 ms wall, 6.109 -\u003e 6.091 ms kernel). Do not attribute the five-sample binary_trees timing gain to this change: its lowered HIR is unchanged.\n\nEvidence retained locally in /private/tmp/zyntax-receiver-guards (raw JSON, HIR summary, exact measured patch, build and phase-trace logs, binary hashes). The NUC checkout and all original changes were restored and verified; temporary benchmark artifacts were removed.\n\nValidation: cargo test --offline -j 2 -p zyntax_lua --release --features llvm-backend -- --test-threads=2: 110 test functions passed, including all 46 conformance groups and 4 official-suite checks. Existing known failures remain explicitly tracked; no baseline or KNOWN_FAILURES edits. Added 15 fixtures pinned against Lua 5.4.9, exercised with LLVM disabled and enabled, and structural tests for retained/removed receiver-nil guards. cargo clippy --offline -j 2 -p zyntax_lua --features llvm-backend --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt --all --check and git diff --check passed. Source changes stay in zyntax_lua, so the TS official-corpus rule is not triggered.","files":null}]}